format session-log-v1
stamp 20260825.195437
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title Two piers took the same row number, and the send found it
prompt Same turn, second increment: the send to xykj61 refused the fast-forward, so the round continued into a merge
think The push to origin carried dcef21f03a and closed an eleven-commit gap. The push to xykj61 refused: that remote had moved eight commits ahead while this lap ran, from the same base 5b94dd2d69.
think Read their tree before merging rather than after. Two facts changed the round. Keaton granted the seed force-push word on the macOS bench at 20260825.181028 -- ONE publish, on the next cadence lap whose four gates read green under the %225 wall, routed to this pier because the script and its credential live here -- and granted the FAST/COLD ruling in the same word. Neither is this lap: the rota row is 1 rather than 0, so the cadence lap has not come and the grant waits where it was left.
think The second fact is the red. Their ledger books %226 at 20260825.183029 for two guards speaking one host's awk dialect; mine books %226 at 20260825.183336 for five guards red across a signed send. Both trees agreed at 5b94dd2d69, both allocated by reading the spine off disk and adding one, both spine guards read GREEN, and neither could see the other. A guard that proves a spine whole proves it whole per tree.
think Resolved by stamp order, which is the ledger's own ordering: the earlier row keeps %226 and this pier's three shift to %227, %228, %229. The sweep repointed every citation in the ledger, the card, and three tool comments. Two dated commit bodies keep the numbers they wrote -- accrete-never-break -- and %230 is the row that explains them.
think %230 stands OPEN as a gate rather than closed. Fetch-before-book, a per-pier band, and a stamp-keyed row numbered at merge are three shapes, and choosing among them is Keaton's word beside the three-real-MOX design already booked on the card.
obs Caught in my own hand before it shipped: %230's stamp was first written 20260825.200112, a number no clock had produced. Read TZ=America/New_York date and wrote 20260825.195437 instead. It never left the working tree, and it is recorded here because a fabricated stamp is the one kind of number this tree cannot audit after the fact.
obs Five conflicts resolved: the two generated pages by regenerating from a clean base, the ledger by stamp order, the index by stamp order, and the card by taking this pier's condense and folding in both grants.
obs The card holds at 24,571 bytes against 24,576, the ledger at 1..230 with no gaps or duplicates, and both remotes will carry the same spine once the merge lands.
loom merge theirs=8 mine=1 conflicts=5 rows_renumbered=3 citations_swept=6
loom reds_spine rows=230 gaps_or_dupes=0 headline=generated
file construction/REDS.md row %230 OPEN as a gate; %227-%229 renumbered; their %226 kept at its stamp
file construction/ITINERARY.md both grants folded in, Now refreshed, 24,571 bytes
file session-logs/README.md six rows merged in stamp order
recommend check-in Keaton's word on how two piers allocate the next row number -- %230 -- and the granted seed publish waits for a cadence lap
